.TH AGENT-WORKFLOW-BENCHMARK 1 "2026-09-21" "agent-workflow 0.11.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-workflow-benchmark \- optional paired comparative benchmark capability
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-workflow benchmark suite-export
.I DESTINATION
.RI [ --benchmark-id " ID" ]
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark structured-value-smoke-export
.I DESTINATION
.RI [ --agent-class " NAME" ]
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark readiness
.I SPEC
.B --executor
.I EXECUTOR
.RI [ --policy " POLICY" ]
.RI [ --execution-only ]
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark plan
.I SPEC
.B --repo
.I REPO
.B --executor
.I EXECUTOR
.RI [ --repetitions " N" ]
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark run
.I RUN_PLAN
.RI [ --execution-only ]
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark score
.I RUN_PLAN
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark report
.I RUN_PLAN
.br
.B agent-workflow benchmark verify
.I RUN_PLAN
.SH DESCRIPTION
The benchmark command is contributed by the optional agent-workflow-benchmark plugin. It creates paired, isolated comparative runs with sealed treatment identity, source identity, executor/model cohort, timing, usage, scoring, and evidence. Agent-Workflow core retains lifecycle and evidence authority; benchmark policy does not change Agent Run acceptance authority.
.SH BM3 STRUCTURED STUDY
Benchmark 0.3 adds a structured development comparison between structured-direct/v1 and agent-workflow-full/v1. Both treatments use the structured workflow profile; only the candidate executes through the Agent-Workflow Agent Run lifecycle. This isolates lifecycle/orchestration overhead from structured prompt discipline.
.PP
The repository helper runs the complete BM3 development path:
.PP
.nf
bash scripts/run-bm3-structured.sh --help
bash scripts/run-bm3-structured.sh --root /path/to/bm3 --repetitions 1
.fi
.PP
The helper performs a pre-treatment TypeSafe/Jev routing qualification, then readiness, paired planning/execution, machine scoring, descriptive reporting, granular timing capture, and self-contained evidence collection. The paired treatments are expected to emit zero additional TypeSafe calls.
.SH TIMING
Per-phase evidence preserves wall time, executor-active time, provider usage, and a timing_breakdown. The Agent-Workflow arm additionally records delegate-call time, terminal wait, benchmark evidence-copy time, derived host overhead, and copied terminal-pipeline section timings.
.PP
Each Agent-Workflow run may contain sealed terminal-timing.json with host-side timing for completion collection, assignment close, task-result collection, post-policy collection, patch capture, provider-evidence normalization, policy evaluation, provenance update, final-status writing, execution-evidence generation, and the pre-seal terminal total.
.SH TYPESAFE AUDIT
BM3 first exercises the real comparative routing boundary on the exported phase prompts and writes typesafe-semantic-qualification.json plus redacted agent-workflow/typesafe-api-call/v2 JSONL under its artifact root. This qualification occurs outside both benchmark arms; treatment execution must add zero semantic-routing calls. Raw semantic request/response bodies are private evidence and must not be published; public summaries should use counts, durations, hashes, primitive coverage, disagreement, and calibration statistics.
.SH EVIDENCE
Benchmark 0.2.9 and later archive the smoke root, coordinator run, and referenced treatment arm evidence roots so arm.json, provider telemetry, Git evidence, scores, and phase receipts remain self-contained. The collector refuses an archive if the active TYPESAFE_API_KEY value appears in collected files.
.SH INTERPRETATION
Development runs are descriptive. A machine score may be diagnostically useful even when missing visual or human evidence makes the pair ineligible for a composite or winner claim. Do not generalize from one paired repetition.
.SH INSTALLATION
The benchmark command requires the agent-workflow-benchmark plugin enabled in the same environment as Agent-Workflow. The complete local stack may be updated, built, installed, and verified with:
.PP
.nf
bash scripts/build-install-all.sh --venv /path/to/.venv
.fi
.PP
Normal full-stack installation first runs a fail-closed git pull --ff-only across the configured stack repositories. Use --no-pull for an offline/reproducible build, --pull-only to update repositories without building, and --verify-only for a non-mutating installed-stack audit.
.SH SEE ALSO
